cartridge "RADS/Bill Chin/1988"

' RADS
' A ColecoVision game based on "RADs", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, February 1988
' (Commodore 64, 4,650 bytes of machine language started with SYS 2061;
' the disk and the program do not name the author). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: ionic storms have spun the relay spindles of a deep-space
' communication network out of line. You are a RADS repair droid. Fly to a
' spindle, hold the button and push the stick right to wind its gauge up,
' left to wind it down. Every spindle must end exactly full or exactly
' empty, and as many full ones as the key at the bottom of the screen
' shows. Storm sparks hunt you: one touch and the droid drops out of the
' field. Shoot them with the button away from the spindles. The field
' changes for each of nine levels, then they come round again.
'
' From the original (orig/rads.dis, the machine code; orig/notes.txt):
' the nine spindle layouts ($1800/$1809); a spindle's gauge of 32 steps
' that starts at 9-23 (random 1-15 + 8) and the key square of each
' spindle drawn full or empty at random ($0D9D-$0DFE); the level is clear
' when every gauge is 0 or 32 and the number of empty ones matches the key
' ($0E60: the key counts, not the order); one gauge step every 32 - 2 x
' level ticks, twice as fast with the droid on the middle of the spindle
' ($1085), the stick's last left/right choosing the way ($09D7); the
' droid's speeds 39 + level across and 21 + level up and down ($094C; it
' stops when the stick is let go); bolts along the droid's facing, held
' button, at most a few, stopped by anything they meet ($11E8, $124F);
' six storm cells, each new one from the bottom row: an ion orb with
' chance (32 - 2 x level)/256 else a spark, a scrambler instead of an orb
' and a hunter instead of a spark with chance level/15 ($14F4); speeds
' random 2-9 + kind + level ($14CB); sparks and hunters re-aim at the
' droid every 40 ticks (hunters every 20) ($1450); a cell on a spindle
' bounces off one time in eight, and a scrambler then spins that gauge to
' a random 2-30 and turns into a hunter ($13CE); a bolt turns a spark into
' a harmless dud that sinks, a scrambler into a hunter, a hunter into a
' spark after six bolts, and bursts an orb, which slows the storm for a
' while ($133D, $12F9); a spark or hunter that touches the droid knocks it
' down: it falls to the bottom and a life is lost, the droid comes back
' at the top for a moment out of harm's way ($1150, $1621, $15E4); four
' droids; a level clear scores one point per spindle (here 100).
'
' Liberties: the C64's 40 x 22 field (which wrapped round left to right)
' is 28 x 20 tiles here with side walls: cells bounce off them and hunt
' straight at the droid; spindles are 3 tiles wide, not 4, their columns
' scaled 25/32 (levels 7 and 8 re-spaced to stay apart); the speeds are
' worked out from the C64's ticks (about 2.8 a frame) into pixels a frame,
' with the droid's left/right speed scaled to the narrower field; cells
' pass through each other (the C64 bounced them); 3 + level/2 cells at once
' (6 from level 7; the C64 always had 6, too many for a human-paced player
' on the narrower field); the rare speed-up of the
' storm in the middle of a level is left out; the C64's time-slowing
' busy loop after an orb is a 10-second half-speed storm here; the
' droid comes back with 1.5 seconds out of harm's way (the C64 about 0.8);
' scores: 100 a spindle at a level clear (the C64: 1), plus 10 for a dud,
' 25 for a hunter brought down to a spark and 50 for an orb (the C64 gave
' nothing for shooting); one droid (the C64's DROID TEAM and COMPETITION
' need a second player); spindles turn green once full or empty; the
' level clear flashes no colours (the C64 flashed every frame); GAME OVER
' then the title (the C64 asked CONTINUE, RESTART or QUIT).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Stick: fly. Button (either), on a
' spindle: hold and push right to fill, left to empty. Button elsewhere:
' shoot a bolt the way the droid faces. Title: up/down and a button
' (START, HOW TO PLAY: three pages, CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

